FAM (alpha)
============================
File - App monitor for Linux
2026 @ juliandroske

FAM can monitor certain files operations performed by processes and programs,
and eventually detect garbage files created by programs that are no longer
exist (uninstalled).

Inspired by HDAS [1], FAM borns as a lightweight alternative based on
fanotify (with netlink) instead of eBPF, as well as being written in C (C99)
instead of Rust. (And without AI.)

Requires Linux kernel 5.9+ with fanotify enabled (CONFIG_FANOTIFY=y), also
root access for tracking file changes.

Expects breaking changes before first release. For current limitations and
bugs, see BUGS below.


BUILD
-----

Requires a C99 compiler and SQLite (>= 3.35.0) development files.

With GNU make:
  | $ make
or simply with any C99 compiler:
  | $ cc -o fam onemain.c -lsqlite3


USAGE (main)
------------

For all command blocks in this section:
  | # The command needs the root privilege
  | $ Runnable as any user, will success with appropriate permissions

fam [options...] <command> [command options...]

FAM consists of a daemon that runs in background to keep the track of file
operations, and a command line tool for users to analyse them; they do not
depend on each other because no IPC are engaged but a shared SQLite database
is used. To start the daemon and monitor all file changes on the system:
  | # fam monitor
To focus on certain directories, run something like:
  | # fam monitor $HOME/.config $HOME/.local
Multiple daemons can be run at the same time as long as their targets are not
overlapped.

To stop the daemon, press Ctrl+C or do a 'kill <FAM PID>' and FAM will
gracefully exit soon as possible. Doing it again will trigger a force
termination. If still not working, try 'kill -9 <FAM PID>'.

Then, to see recorded file changes:
  | $ fam list
Or organize the result by programs:
  | $ fam show
Executable paths or packages can also be passed like:
  | $ fam show /bin/dd coreutils
  | $ fam show dpkg busybox dpkg:bash
where the "dpkg:" prefix indicates the package "bash" comes from dpkg package
manager instead of other sources.

You may "mask" certain executables or packages so that FAM will not treat
them as the origin program who did the change to a file:
  | $ fam config --mask true dpkg:bash
After this, FAM will look for and record the nearest parent process which is
not masked.

To show files associated with uninstalled programs:
  | $ fam orphans
options include '--delete' to remove these files (if exist), and '--forget'
to let FAM forget all of these files. Note that FAM will not actively
synchronize with current package managers, nor check executable existence, so
it is suggested to do a manual scan in advance:
  | $ fam update

For full usage, run
  | $ fam --help
or see the same help text in main.c.


USAGE (fatrace)
---------------

FAM can be used as a standalone file monitor, similar to fatrace but with
fatrace/issue#52 fixed, like:
  | # fam monitor -n
Output options still apply. No database will be opened with this command.


BUGS
----

Beyond current tests, FAM might not be able to catch every process and
therefore lose minor amount of information. For details see NOTES below.

Several filesystems are not supported (refers to FAN_MARK_FILESYSTEM) for the
lack of fsid capability,

fanotify(7)
fsid
> Note that some filesystems (e.g., fuse(4)) report zero fsid. In these cases,
> it is not possible to use fsid to associate the event with a specific
> filesystem instance, so monitoring different filesystem instances that
> report zero fsid with the same fanotify group is not supported.

including certain virtual filesystems such as procfs and sysfs, and some real
ones (exfat, ...).

FAM will not monitor new mountpoints after the daemon has already started.

Current design can contain race conditions as there are neither locks nor IPC
between processes. A possible fix is to aggregate separated SQL queries into
one single step so that operations can be protected by ACID properties. This
should be fixed before first release.


NOTES
-----

The fanotify API reports PID with no further process information, causing the
bug in fatrace [2] (issue#52) where executable path is not fetchable because
the process exits immediately before fatrace receives it.

To overcome this, FAM integrates process tracking based on netlink, also
implemented in forkstat [3], which is, however, neither reliable as written
in forkstat's README.md:

> Note that forkstat uses the Linux netlink connector to gather process
> activity and this may miss events if the system is overly busy. Netlink
> connector also requires root privilege.

while netlink reports never promise to precede fanotify events if a program
calls open() right after exec().

A preivous attempt was initializing fanotify with FAN_CLASS_CONTENT (or
FAN_CLASS_PRE_CONTENT) and marking directories with a permission event
FAN_OPEN_PERM to ensure process information are fetched before actual
operations happen, as fatrace/issue#52 suggested, so that the process
tracking or 'pstrack' can be abandoned while supporting older linux back to
2.6; it works perfectly, however disables the capability of receiving file
creation, movement and deletion events (impossible with FAN_CREATE, ...
while there are no such permission events as FAN_CREATE_PERM, ...), as
manuals imply:

fanotify_mark(2)
FAN_OPEN_PERM
> An fanotify file descriptor created with FAN_CLASS_PRE_CONTENT or
> FAN_CLASS_CONTENT is required.
FAN_CREATE
> An fanotify group that identifies filesystem objects by file handles
> is required.

fanotify_init(2)
FAN_REPORT_FID
> This value allows the receipt of events which contain additional
> information about the underlying filesystem object correlated to
> an event.
(enables FAN_CREATE)
> The use of FAN_CLASS_CONTENT or FAN_CLASS_PRE_CONTENT is not permitted
> with this flag and will result in the error EINVAL.
(breaks FAN_OPEN_PERM)

which is critical for associating certain file operations with processes.

On the database (SQLite) part, to get to know query failures, FAM for now
utilises the non-standard "returning" clause introduced in SQLite "3.35.0
(2021-03-12)". The Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) mode is also enabled to avoid
write failures (SQLITE_BUSY), gaining more concurrency control as FAM allows
at least two processes (daemon + cli) running simultaneously by design, but
the choice is majorly based on two fixes in SQLite in 3.11.0 and 3.22.0 [4];
under the hood SQLite acquires a region of shared memory (SHM) across
processes, so the whole program still uses a little bit IPC internally.


